      "math_academy_id": "q-74370",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}+2?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242844/assets/1cc1a1f5fa2bf98d700bf0c8d8e0630a4fda0540b8715865ee72d34b17049b8a.png)\n\nSince we have $y=-\\frac{1}{x}\\,+\\,2,$ we need to translate the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ *up* by $2$ units.\n\nNow, since the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is translated up by $2$ units, the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ is also translated up by $2$ units. So, the equation of the new horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242844/assets/fa40fe0970b99770dfa6f228f4ed7b49db74d03c37162879b3458d83d340e7c1.png)",
